ownership and\n   scope (§3.4 item 6).\n3. **A carried live claim**: not carried; the window's keys are the\n   membership claim and are validated by recomputation (§4.2).\n4. **Tail effects**: routed directly through `K`; the engine seam's\n   `restore-fn(base, entry, value)` needs no current activation, so no\n   trampoline; the closed `tail` state and the semantic restore helper\n   are specified in §2.4.\n\nNothing remains open in this document before freeze. The one item\nthat belongs to the phase-3 implementation brief rather than to the\ndesign is the choice of the independent reference implementation that\n§8.1's `L` is checked against.\n\n## 12. What this does not change\n\n`:yin.safepoint/pc` and `:yin.safepoint/engine`, replaces\n`:stack-effect` with the per-pc **def and use sets** and `L(pc)` derived\nfrom A (§8.1), and keeps `:yin.safepoint/layout` as a map from physical\nlocation to virtual register. The reconstruction obligation is: for an\n`act` state, produce every register in `saved(pc, rd) = L(pc) − {rd}`\n(never the not-yet-produced destination), `E`, `K` with each frame's\nvalidated window and `rd`, the delivery record, the pending state, and\nthe captured store context; for a `tail` state, produce `K` with its\nvalidated frames, the delivery record and the pending state, and **no**\ncurrent window or `E`. A physical-slot map alone does not discharge it\nwhen values are spilled, rematerialized, or shared between locations;\nthe engine owes the values, however it kept them. `lift(lower(frame)) =\nframe` holds for admitted canonical frames, as today.\n\n## 5. Scheduler, park, resume, FFI, modules\n\nUnchanged in meaning (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.4, §3.5; UCF §7.6.3):\nthe wait set, `:parked`, the fresh-name counter, the FFI pair, the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-09 18:38:19 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-10 01:38:19 Asia/Bangkok\n\nReconstruction, explicit park classification, conservative allocation overlap, and CFG reachability wording now pass. The revised terminal-outcome model still has three freeze blockers.\n\n| Severity | file:line | Evidence | Correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| High — specification contradiction | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:428` | §3.1 now says resume mints a destination, but §3.3 still says “resume mints nothing”; line 438 likewise excludes resume. Canonical numbering therefore has two incompatible rules. | Update §3.3 to mint a conceptual destination for resume, retaining the existing arm-destination reuse rule. |\n| High — outcome/definition contradiction | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:375` | Terminal outcome is equated with a never-defined destination. A tail call still contains a syntactic `rd`; an enclosing call after a terminal operand also contains its destination definition in unreachable layout syntax. Rule 7 and §3.2 do not distinguish syntactic definitions from reachable writes consistently. | Define terminal as **no normal result on that path**, rather than no syntactic definition. Distinguish minted IDs, syntactic destination occurrences, and runtime writes. Make outcome composition conditional on children continuing; explicitly handle terminal tests and mixed result/terminal arms. |\n| High — incomplete allocation | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md:736` | Every virtual register requires an interval starting at its first definition, but resume’s newly minted destination has no definition. A wholly terminal body can nevertheless reference it in its structural return/halt. | Pin how definition-less IDs are lowered: assign deterministic structural-only slots, or specify another deterministic rewriting policy. Ensure every emitted native operand is